course: dictionary
lesson: 12043
title: Talking around the point
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Circumlocution means using lots of words to say something that could be said simply. It is a long way round to say a short thing.
explain: People often use it to avoid saying the direct thing. «The vase is not entirely in one piece» is a circumlocution. It means the vase is broken, but the long words hide that.
explain: Teachers in English lessons ask you to avoid it in your writing, because it hides the meaning. Say what you mean clearly: «The vase is broken.»
fact: Circumlocution means using many words to say something simply.
fact: A circumlocution is one roundabout phrase, such as «not entirely truthful».
fact: «Not entirely truthful» is a circumlocution for «lying».
fact: Circumlocution is a formal word, so you will meet it more in writing and exams than in everyday chat.
fact: Good writing says what it means clearly and in as few words as it needs.
exercise: «Instead of saying "the test was hard", Ruby wrote "the examination posed considerable difficulties for those present".» What is this kind of writing called? | A rhyme | A question | A short, clear sentence | *Circumlocution
exercise: Which version says it simply? | It was not quite the case that he had been honest | He was economical with the truth | *He told us a lie | He was not entirely truthful with us
exercise: Why do teachers warn against circumlocution in writing? | *Because it hides the meaning | Because it uses too few words | Because it is always rude | Because it is too short
test: Which word means using many words to say something that could be said simply? | Rhyme | *Circumlocution | Alliteration | Brevity
test: Which plain sentence says the same as «Grandad has gone to a better place»? | Grandad has moved house | Grandad is on holiday | Grandad has gone shopping | *Grandad has died
exam: Which is the best reason to avoid circumlocution in an essay? | It makes your essay too short to count | It is the only way to show good manners | *It hides what you mean, so your reader may miss your point | It means the same as a rhyme
